    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The core of the libertarian defense of absolute property rights is **natural rights theory, strongly rooted in the Lockean tradition of self-ownership**.\n\nThis perspective argues that because an individual inherently \"owns\" their own body and the product of their *uncoerced* labor, the resulting property is a direct, unalienable extension of that self.\n\nTherefore, any claim by the state to take that property for the \"common good,\" even for a \"fairer outcome,\" is seen as **the ultimate act of aggressive force against the individual being the ultimate moral wrong, fundamentally violating the right to self-determination and liberty that is the bedrock of all other rights.",
      "source": "target",
